this crowd is protesting after 20 days  waiting for food assistance it's hard to  make out her shouts in dialect but her  desperation is clear  the Mafia exploit the vulnerable in  times of crisis reports it's happening  already in the same region of Campania a  local politician took this video of a  post-office queue it may appear normal  but a self-appointed security guard is  demanding money from people at the door  Rocco Rawat olam lost his construction  company to the Mafia in the last  financial crash it later led to this  raid after Rocco infiltrated the Kimura  gang resulting in the arrest of 29  members mobsters who'd offered help with  cash flow then seized control and the  poor are growing more vulnerable food  banks have seen a 40% increase in people  across the country needing help and the  biggest demand is in the south where  poverty is rife 3.7 million Italians  work in the black economy their income  not backed by any government scheme so  they need help  400 million euros has been allocated to  food assistance billions more to helping  businesses but red tape in Italy means  that help could come too late and the  Mafia could be there sooner  in companies' capital maples reports the  Camorra gang is giving food to people  who've lost their jobs and cash to  struggling businesses one in two shops  here now at risk of bankruptcy former  prosecutor and mayor of naples says  they've so far prevented what he calls a  criminal infection but warns the Mafia  is always ready to exploit those in need  [Music]  the funeral of the brother of a former  mafia boss in Sicily earlier this month  shows a crowd flouting lockdown rules  certainly suggesting a sense of  confidence from a crime gang who knows  who's in control Sally Oakwood sky  newsroom
 

